\section{Definitions}

$R$ denotes a rational function of its arguments.

PP of an ODE := its general solution has no movable critical singularities
(synonym = has only fixed critical singularities).

Meromorphic := the only singularities at a finite distance are poles.

Caveat: PP always deals with the \textit{general} solution,
Nevanlinna most often considers only \textit{particular} solutions.

% ============================================================================
% ============================================================================
\section{First order, autonomous, binomial ODEs (Briot and Bouquet)}

Class of ODEs
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\left(\frac{\D u}{\D x}\right)^n = R(u), n \in \mathbb{N}.
\label{eqBinomial}
\end{eqnarray}
Remark: any nonconstant particular solution of (\ref{eqBinomial}) is the general solution.

\textbf{Theorem} (Briot and Bouquet  \cite[\S 247--248]{BriotBouquet})
Class (\ref{eqBinomial}) and meromorphic $\Longrightarrow$
\textit{modulo} an homography on $u$, $9$ allowed values of $(n,R)$,
characterized by the diophantine equation
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\forall j:\ r_j=1-\frac{1}{n_j},
\sum r_j=2, n_j \in \mathbb{Z} \hbox{ (possibly infinite)},
\\ & &
\frac{\D u}{\D x} = \prod_{j=1}^4 (u-a_j)^{r_j}.
\label{eqBB-Diophante}
\end{eqnarray}
See Table A.1 in The PH 2020 page 282, reproduced here.

The converse is true: any ODE in this finite list has a meromorphic general solution,
more precisely elliptic or degenerate.

\tabcolsep=1.5truemm
\tabcolsep=0.5truemm

