\section{Second order, first degree}

Class of ODEs
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\frac{\D^2 u}{\D x^2} = F\left(\frac{\D u}{\D x},u;x \right),
\label{eqOrder2-degree1}
\end{eqnarray}
$F$ rational in $\D u / \D x$, algebraic in $u$, analytic in $x$.

\textbf{Theorem} (Painlev\'e 1900, 1902)
Class (\ref{eqOrder2-degree1}) and PP $\Longrightarrow$
\begin{enumerate}
\item
As a function of $\D u / \D x$, $F$ is a polynomial of degree at most two,
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\frac{\D^2 u}{\D x^2} = L(u,x) \left(\frac{\D u}{\D x}\right)^2 + M(u,x) \frac{\D u}{\D x} + N(u,x).
\label{eqOrder2-degree1-Riccati}
\end{eqnarray}
\item
As a function of $u$, $L$ is a sum of at most 4 simple poles,
\begin{eqnarray}
& & % prod_{j=1}^4 (u-a_j)^{r_j}.
L(u,x) = \sum_{j=1}^4 \frac{r_j}{u-a_j(x)},
\end{eqnarray}
with the same constraints (\ref{eqBB-Diophante}) than in the BB list.
See Painlev\'e 1900  Tableau (T) section 10 page 216.

\item
The only singularities $u$ of the functions $M(u,x)$ and $N(u,x)$ are among those of $L(u,x)$.

\item
All the degrees of all the involved rational functions are bounded,
which makes the problem finite.

\end{enumerate}


\textbf{Conjecture} (Nevanlinna people, see e.g.~Liao and Ye 2005 page 283)
(Subset of class (\ref{eqOrder2-degree1}) with $F$ rational in its three arguments)
and (existence of a particular nonrational meromorphic solution)
$\Longrightarrow$ (\ref{eqOrder2-degree1-Riccati})
with $L,M,N$ rational functions of $u$ and $x$.

\textit{Remark}.
There is a huge gap between P 1900 and this conjecture.



\textbf{Theorem} (Liao and Ye 2005 page 283)
(Subset of class (\ref{eqOrder2-degree1-Riccati}) with $L,M,N$ independent of $x$ and rational in $u$)
and
(existence of a particular nonrational meromorphic solution of Nevanlinna order higher than two)
$\Longrightarrow$
exactly the finite list of Painlev\'e 1902.


\textit{Remark}.
Error in (17), $A$ cannot be arbitrary, as displayed in Liao and Ye page 300 line 2.


\textit{Remark}.
The proof that all cases (10)--(18) do exist is already in P 1902 + Gambier 1910.


% ============================================================================
% ============================================================================
\section{Third order, first degree}

Class of ODEs
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\frac{\D^3 u}{\D x^3} = F\left(\frac{\D^2 u}{\D x^2},\frac{\D u}{\D x},u;x \right),
\label{eqOrder3-degree1}
\end{eqnarray}
$F$ rational in $\D^2 u / \D x^2$ and $\D u / \D x$, algebraic in $u$, analytic in $x$.


\textbf{Theorem} (Painlev\'e 1900 section 34 page 252.)
Class (\ref{eqOrder3-degree1}) and PP $\Longrightarrow$
\begin{enumerate}
\item
As a function of $\D^2 u / \D x^2$, $F$ is a polynomial of degree at most two,
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\frac{\D^3 u}{\D x^3} = L\left(\frac{\D u}{\D x},u,x\right) \left(\frac{\D^2 u}{\D x^2}\right)^2
                       +M\left(\frac{\D u}{\D x},u,x\right)       \frac{\D^2 u}{\D x^2}
											+ N\left(\frac{\D u}{\D x},u,x\right).
\label{eqOrder3-degree1-Riccati}
\end{eqnarray}
\item
As a function of $\D u / \D x$, $L$ is a sum of at most 4 simple poles
as listed in P 1900 page 253.

\item
The only singularities $u$ of the functions $M(u,x)$ and $N(u,x)$ are among those of $L(u,x)$.

\item
(P 1900 p 257)
``For $n=-2$ and $b=0$ (Schwarzian case) the singlevalued functions $y(x)$ defined
by the system (53) constitute the class of automorphic functions
(Fuchsian and Kleinean)''.

``The resolution of the prelimlinary problem which I just mentioned
presents rather serious difficulties.''

\item
($n=-2$, Chazy 1911, about these difficulties)
Page 330 the whole section 4 is devoted to the Schwarzian case.
``The value $n=-2$ [Schwarzian case] is quite exceptional.'',
with a quite interesting footnote.

Page 331 middle [$h$ denotes the number of poles of his auxiliary function $b(y)$]
``For $h \ge 4$ ($h$ is not bounded), except \dots,
the singlevalued functions are Fuchsian or Kleinean functions. \dots''

\item
$n=-2$, limitation of all degrees (much more difficult, only finally settled by Chazy 1918)
All the degrees of all the involved rational functions are bounded,
which makes the problem finite.

\end{enumerate}


% ============================================================================
% ============================================================================
\section{Third order, first degree, Schwarzian case}

This corresponds to $n=-2$ in P 1900 page 253.


Class of ODEs
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\frac{\D^3 u}{\D x^3} = \frac{3}{2}
\frac{\left(\frac{\D^2 u}{\D x^2}\right)^2}{\frac{\D u}{\D x} + a_2(x) u^2 + a_1(x) u + a_0(x)}
 +M\left(\frac{\D u}{\D x},u,x\right)       \frac{\D^2 u}{\D x^2}
											+ N\left(\frac{\D u}{\D x},u,x\right).
\label{eqOrder3-degree1-Schwarzian}
\end{eqnarray}

Definition
$S(u,x):=\left\lbrace u;x\right\rbrace$ (Schwarzian).

Restricted class of autonomous ODEs considered by Ishizaki
\begin{eqnarray}
& &
\left(S(u,x)\right)^n=R(u).
\label{eqOrder3-degree1-Schwarzian-autonomous}
\end{eqnarray}

Theorem (Ishizaki 1991).

(Class (\ref{eqOrder3-degree1-Schwarzian-autonomous}))
and
(existence of a particular meromorphic solution)

$\Longrightarrow$

(a finite list of $R(f)$ in one-to-one correspondence with the list
of Briot and Bouquet),
listed \textit{modulo} homographies
in two sublists in Liao and Wu arXiv:2103.01393v1:
(i) list (3)-(8) if $u$ is nonrational,
(ii) list of Remark 1 if $u$ is rational.

Now, my main point.
The elliptic solutions listed in Liao and Wu arXiv:2103.01393v1
\textit{must} be in one-to-one correspondence with the elliptic solutions
of BB recalled in Table \ref{TableBB}.

This implies, \textit{modulo} homographies:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
The $u(z)$ in Theorem 5 is $\wp'(z)$.

\item
The $u(z)$ in Theorem 6 is $\wp^2(z)$.

\end{enumerate}


